  "title": "Erdős Problem #679",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "Let $\\epsilon>0$ and $\\omega(n)$ count the number of distinct prime factors of $n$. Are there infinitely many values of $n$ such that $ \\omega(n-k) < (1+\\epsilon)\\frac{\\log k}{\\log\\log k} $ for all $k<n$ which are sufficiently large depending on $\\epsilon$ only?\nCan one show the stronger version with $ \\omega(n-k) < \\frac{\\log k}{\\log\\log k}+O(1) $ is false?",
    "sha256": "af041ca41c96ecae1f1e9a67472a89aee8724f5b6cfbf8bea770cca3ad48eeb7",
